+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls**
+*   **Poor Interpolation with Standard MLPs:** Using the same neural network architecture as NeRF (a 5D radiance field MLP) to parameterize a light field (4D ray-space) leads to poor interpolation quality for view synthesis. This is because learning a function on 4D ray space from partial 2D slices is significantly more challenging than learning 3D density [S1, S1, unofficial].
+*   **Convex Hull Assumption:** The method assumes radiance is constant along rays. This holds when the convex hull of the scene geometry does not contain any viewpoints used for rendering. If this assumption is violated, the single-evaluation-per-pixel model may produce artifacts [S1, S1, unofficial].
+*   **Sparse Input Interpolation:** The embedding network alone does not interpolate well in sparser input sequences. Local light fields are required to mitigate large depth and texture changes [S1, S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Speed:** Supports rendering with a single network evaluation per pixel for small baseline light fields, which is significantly faster than NeRF's hundreds of evaluations per ray [S1, unofficial]. For larger baselines or sparse scenes with local fields, it requires a few evaluations per pixel [S1, unofficial].
-- **Memory:** Maintains a small memory footprint compared to volumetric representations [S1, unofficial].
-- **Quality:** Achieves state-of-the-art view synthesis quality on dense forward-facing datasets like the Stanford Light Field dataset [S1, unofficial]. For sparse inputs, it achieves results competitive with NeRF-based approaches [S1, unofficial].
-- **Strengths:** Fast, memory efficient, excels at modeling complicated view dependence (e.g., distorted reflections and refractions) [S1, unofficial].
-- **Limitations:** Learning light fields directly with standard NeRF architectures leads to poor interpolation [S1, unofficial]. Sparse scenes require a more complex setup with local light fields [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Speed:** Supports rendering with a single network evaluation per pixel for small baseline light fields, and only a few evaluations per pixel for larger baselines. This is significantly faster than NeRF, which requires hundreds of evaluations per ray [S1, S1, unofficial].
+*   **Memory:** Maintains a small memory footprint compared to volumetric representations [S1, S1, unofficial].
+*   **Quality:** Achieves state-of-the-art view synthesis quality on dense forward-facing datasets (e.g., Stanford Light Fields) [S1, S1, unofficial]. For sparse inputs, it achieves results competitive with NeRF-based approaches [S1, S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Fast, memory efficient, excels at modeling complicated view-dependent effects (e.g., distorted reflections and refractions) [S1, S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Relies on the assumption that radiance is constant along rays. Performance on sparse inputs requires the complexity of local light fields [S1, S1, unofficial].
+*   **Comparison to AutoInt:** Unlike AutoInt, which predicts integrated radiance along ray segments and uses subdivision but has lower accuracy than NeRF due to approximation of the nested volume rendering integral, this approach leads to comparable performance to NeRF [S1, S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-- **Origin:** The method was introduced in "Learning Neural Light Fields with Ray-Space Embedding Networks" by Benjamin et al. [S1, unofficial].
-- **History:** It addresses the limitations of prior neural volumetric representations, which often have large memory footprints and struggle with view-dependent effects [S1, unofficial]. It builds on the concept of light fields as integrals of radiance fields [S1, unofficial].
-- **Usage:** Used for view synthesis in computer vision and graphics, particularly for forward-facing scenes [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Origin:** The method was introduced in the paper "Learning Neural Light Fields with Ray-Space Embedding Networks" by Benjamin et al. [S1, S1, unofficial].
+*   **History:** The work was done while the first author was an intern at Meta [S1, S1, unofficial]. It addresses the limitations of prior volumetric representations, which often have large memory footprints and struggle with view-dependent effects, and the challenges of learning light fields directly from 2D slices using standard coordinate-based MLPs [S1, S1, unofficial].
+*   **Usage:** Used for view synthesis on forward-facing scenes, both dense (Stanford Light Fields) and sparse (Real Forward-Facing) [S1, S1, unofficial].
 
